From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: shikosu <teovidal01@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 16:32:14 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] horizon on linux: a signal wakes the threads that wait for
 it, not all of them

Every kernel wait shared one condition and every signal did notify_all, so a signal woke the 38 or so guest threads, which then fought for the lock and the GIL: SignalProcessWideKey took 1.85 ms and a thread pool phase of the game's update 1.7 ms. The kernel state keeps one lock; a thread that waits parks on an event of its own (Hle.park), and the unlock, the key signal, the event signal and a thread's exit set the events of the threads concerned only. NX2LINUX_SVC_PROFILE=1 prints the kernel calls that blocked or took over 0.5 ms, by call site. In the opening cinematic: 339 frames in 110 s before, 1407 after.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

Upstream-Status: pending
Reason: NX2Linux S7bis: the game's own threads spent most of their time waking one another through the shared condition
Issue: none yet (NX2Linux, branch swann/jalon-s7bis-xc2)
